{"kind":"assert","text":"This request overstates one of its two consequences, and the overstatement is checkable at main 97ab60c3.\n\nIt says nothing resolves a page's rests_on against the durable log, and concludes that a page may cite an act that does not exist at all while every gate stays green. That second consequence is false at this head. internal/docset/anchor_test.go holds TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, which opens the real workroom with app.Open, folds a snapshot, and errors with \"names no statement in this workroom\" for any anchor that does not resolve. It also errors when the named act resolves to something other than an artifact. So a fabricated or absent identifier is caught today.\n\nThe timing is worth stating because it explains the error rather than excusing it. That gate arrived in commit 777da6a0 on 2026-08-09 at 14:11, and this request was filed on 2026-08-10 at 12:26 — about eighteen hours later. The gate already existed when the request said nothing looked the identifier up.\n\nThe first consequence stands, and it is the real defect. The gate does not ask whether the resolved artifact is retired, so a page anchored to a retired artifact passes green. I measured that yesterday: of 46 pages carrying rests_on, four cited retired artifacts — five anchors in total, across work-loop.md, review.md, deploy-a-resident.md and serve.md. This request names work-loop.md and artifact 7bf40860 specifically, and that is one of the five. My head e9a1c55a, open for review, repairs all four pages, so the evidence this request cites is addressed even though the gate gap it describes is not.\n\nThe fresh-clone constraint this request raises is real and interacts with what is already there. anchor_test.go handles it by calling t.Skipf when no workroom is present, and again when the set names a different genesis than the checkout holds. So the existing gate already degrades rather than failing in a clone without a workroom — which is one of the shapes this request asks someone to choose between, already chosen for the existence check. Whoever takes this should decide whether the same skip is an honest posture for a liveness check, or whether skipping silently is exactly the failure the request is complaining about.\n\nI also filed a second request for this same defect yesterday, 436c8c99, having failed to find this one. I have withdrawn it naming this request as the survivor. Two open requests for one defect is worse than none, because each makes the other look like separate work."}